# Three.js Guide

Rendering is draw-only. Placement, occupancy, validation, and scenario rules must remain outside Three.js.

## Rendering boundaries

- Do not implement business rules, spatial validation, collision detection, or zone logic inside scene or view code.
- Consume documents, typed view models, or spatial results from the spatial layer or application orchestration — not invent them in the renderer.
- Keep mesh/light/material builders in the reusable rendering layer; keep host/plugin wiring out of that layer.
- Do not expose Three.js objects through spatial / document APIs.
- Keep coordinate conversion between spatial data and Three.js objects inside the rendering boundary.

## Resource ownership & lifecycle

- Every GPU resource must have a clear owner and lifecycle.
- Dispose geometries, materials, textures, render targets, controls, and renderers when their owning lifecycle ends.
- Traverse scene groups and dispose owned child resources; do not drop references and rely on garbage collection.
- Do not dispose shared resources from individual consumers.
- Shared asset caches must own cached templates; scene-owned clones dispose with the scene.
- Prevent double-disposal and accidental disposal of resources still in use.
- Clean up event listeners, observers, animation loops, and other external resources during teardown.

## Geometry & materials

- Reuse geometries and materials when multiple objects share appearance or structure.
- Prefer `InstancedMesh` or batched rendering for repeated objects when count warrants it.
- Avoid creating duplicate materials and geometries unnecessarily.
- Avoid unnecessary scene graph nesting.
- Keep material and geometry configuration reusable where appropriate.

## Asset loading

- Prefer compressed GLB (Draco / Meshopt when appropriate).
- Respect model-count and asset-size budgets.
- Scale models to the project’s world-unit conventions.
- Keep orientation and origin conventions consistent (document them in feature docs, not by scattering magic numbers).
- Cache loaded templates and clone them for instances instead of reloading.
- Handle load failures gracefully with suitable fallbacks (procedural geometry is fine).
- Failed or cancelled loads must not leave partially initialized resources behind.

## Coordinate systems

- Use one consistent world-coordinate and orientation convention across rendering.
- Centralize grid ↔ world conversion; do not reimplement it per mesh.
- Do not introduce Three.js types into the spatial / document layer.

## Frame loop & performance

- Avoid allocating expensive objects inside frequently executed frame callbacks.
- Mutate existing objects; do not rebuild scene graphs every frame.
- Prefer one shared clock / animation-loop owner per mount root.
- Skip work for hidden or disposed views.
- Use demand-based rendering when continuous animation is not required.
- Keep light counts low; prefer baked or simplified shadows when visuals allow.
- Avoid unnecessary shadow-map updates and expensive post-processing.
- Cap pixel ratio; prefer quality presets over unbounded device pixel ratios.
- Multiple mount roots must keep independent renderer and lifecycle state.

## Debugging

- Debug grids, helpers, axes, and overlays must be opt-in.
- Debug resources follow the same ownership and disposal rules as production resources.
- Prefer `renderer.info` and simple diagnostic flags over permanent overlays.
- Do not leave debug tooling on in production unless explicitly required.

## Domain fit

- Application views own mount-root lifecycle and host integration.
- Reusable rendering and model-loading stay application-agnostic.
- When structure or feature behavior is unclear, consult project docs — not hard-coded paths in these rules.
